{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_015agz1ZQGCLQauL121qoYw6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The file uses CRLF (`\\r\\n`) line endings and contains proper UTF-8 em dashes. The garbled version doesn't exist in the original file. So when I read it with `encoding='utf-8'` and write it back with `indent=2`, the json.dump is NOT producing the garbled version either... let me look more carefully.\n\nWait, the previous check showed:\n- The original file (after `git checkout`) has `\\u00e2\\u20ac\\u201d` (which are the JSON escape sequences for the bytes of a double-encoded em dash) - that means the file has literal escape sequences `\\u00e2\\u20ac\\u201d` in it, not actual em dash characters!\n\n\n- But when I check the raw bytes, I find 244 occurrences of `\\xe2\\x80\\x94` (proper UTF-8 em dash)\n\nThese two things contradict each other. The git diff is showing me the escaped representation of UTF-8 bytes that got double-encoded somewhere in the JSON. So the file actually contains the proper em dash bytes, but they're being displayed as escape sequences in the diff output.\n\nWhen I reverted with `git checkout`, it went back to the HEAD version which has these UTF-8 bytes stored directly, while my Python script is producing the garbled `â€\"` text. The core issue seems to be that prd.json is storing raw UTF-8 bytes for the em dash, and git diff is just displaying them in escaped form.\n\nLet me trace through what Python actually reads from the file to understand where the mismatch is happening.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":362,"cache_read_input_tokens":51731,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":362},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d904885-7