{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_018Fho1uC5N193JGQoEvCxTN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The file stores `â€\"` which when read as UTF-8 gives `â€\"` (with those being actual characters). But the hex encoding `c3a2e282ace2809d` suggests these are the UTF-8 bytes for:\n- `c3 a2` = â (U+00E2)\n- `e2 82 ac` = € (U+20AC) -- but that's 3 bytes for €\n- Wait, `e2 80 9d` = right double quotation mark (U+201D)\n\n\nSo the file contains mojibake—the original em dash bytes got misinterpreted as UTF-8, creating these garbled characters instead. When Python reads the file with UTF-8 encoding, it correctly interprets those bytes as the Unicode characters `â€\"`, and when it writes them back with `ensure_ascii=False`, it outputs their UTF-8 representation, which is exactly what was already there. I'm trying to figure out what `repr()` is actually showing for the string content. I see the pattern now—`â€\"` is actually Mojibake, the result of UTF-8 bytes being misinterpreted as Windows-1252. The original em dash (`—`, which is `E2 80 94` in UTF-8) got corrupted into those three garbled characters when the encoding got mixed up. When Python reads this corrupted string as UTF-8, it treats each byte sequence as separate Unicode characters, and writing them back with `ensure_ascii=False` outputs their individual UTF-8 encodings, which is why we're seeing that strange hex sequence. So the git diff is showing the Unicode escape sequences `\\u00e2\\u20ac\\u201d` in the minus line because that's how git represents those characters in JSON, but they're actually the same UTF-8 bytes either way — the file hasn't actually changed, just how it's being displayed.\n\nThe real issue is that using `json.dump` with `ensure_ascii=False` converts those escapes to literal UTF-8 characters, which changes the file representation on disk even though the JSON is semantically identical. With 606 lines changed in the diff, this isn't just affecting one field — it's a widespread formatting change across the entire file.\n\nI need to use a text replacement approach instead of parsing and re-serializing the JSON, so I can preserve the original file format while making only the specific changes needed.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1122,"cache_read_input_tokens":52093,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1122},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d904885-7413-494e-8511-9a02e1f067dc","uuid":"e51bbc23-b17d-4637-9a93-fedea9d1476b"}
