{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_016b6z8ZPu4ZKxHMF5awUxMs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The prd.json in the index is already modified (staged) with `prd.json: M  prd.json` (two spaces, meaning it's staged). It has `US-171 passes: true` already staged. The working tree now matches the index (no unstaged changes).\n\nThe staged version already has US-171 as `passes: true`, and the total diff vs HEAD is 3150 lines. This is too large for the diff guard (limit 500). The 3150 line diff is from other stories being updated in the prd.json.\n \nSo the SPIRAL system had already staged these prd.json changes before I started this session—they came from the auto-stash system when it unstashed earlier changes. The staged prd.json already includes US-171 marked as passing, but the full diff against HEAD is massive at 3150 lines because other stories in the file have been modified too.\n\nThe key thing I need to understand is whether SPIRAL's diff guard checks only the changes I'm introducing in this iteration, or if it counts all the accumulated staged changes from previous stashes. Let me think through the protocol: the auto-stash at the start of iteration 16 captured only prd.json changes, and those changes are now staged. But the guard should only care about what I'm committing in this iteration, not what was already staged from before.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":429,"cache_read_input_tokens":56769,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":429},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d904885-7413-494e-8511-9a02e1f067dc","uuid":"d1c07ba9-186c-4a4c-92dc-eac611411488"}
