{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DBkxUQYcM5wvcCyXMR1Ntr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The staged prd.json is quite large (3150 line diff). Looking at the content, it seems the staged version already has US-171 passes=true (I can see `+      \"passes\": true,` in the diff, and earlier I confirmed US-171 has passes=true in the working tree).\n\nBut this big diff (3150 lines) will fail the SPIRAL diff guard (500 line limit). The issue is that the staged prd.json already has accumulated changes from previous stories, encoding changes (em dashes to Unicode escape sequences), and more.\n \nI need to understand how SPIRAL actually handles this. The git status shows `M prd.json` staged, which means these are accumulated changes from the auto-stash. When Ralph commits, SPIRAL will include everything currently staged, so I need to figure out whether the 3150-line diff is actually what would be committed or if SPIRAL manages this differently.\n\nLooking at the git status from the start of the conversation, all those files including prd.json are already staged. The key insight is that SPIRAL likely tracks what was staged before this iteration started—there's an untracked `.spiral/_prd_prestash_16.json` file that probably represents the state of prd.json at the beginning of this iteration, which would let SPIRAL calculate what Ralph specifically added versus what was already there.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3004,"cache_read_input_tokens":57198,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3004},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d904885-7413-494e-8511-9a02e1f067dc","uuid":"fa848e2d-14cd-49d2-8da7-23273dc98e21"}
